---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const publishedAt = '2026-02-24';
---

<Layout
  title="Getting Started"
  description="How to add astro-markdown-for-agents to your Astro project in minutes."
>
  <article>
    <header>
      <h1>Getting Started with astro-markdown-for-agents</h1>
      <p>
        <time datetime={publishedAt}>February 24, 2026</time>
      </p>
    </header>

    <p>
      Adding Markdown-for-agents support to an existing Astro project takes about two
      minutes. This post walks through the complete setup.
    </p>

    <h2>Prerequisites</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>An Astro project (v4 or v5)</li>
      <li>Node.js 18 or later</li>
      <li>
        A server-side adapter if you want the middleware to run in production (e.g.
        <code>@astrojs/cloudflare</code>, <code>@astrojs/node</code>)
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Step 1 — Install the package</h2>

    <pre><code>npm install astro-markdown-for-agents</code></pre>

    <h2>Step 2 — Register the integration</h2>

    <pre><code>// astro.config.mjs
import &#123; defineConfig &#125; from 'astro/config';
import cloudflare from '@astrojs/cloudflare';
import markdownForAgents from 'astro-markdown-for-agents';

export default defineConfig(&#123;
  output: 'server',
  adapter: cloudflare(),
  integrations: [markdownForAgents()],
&#125;);</code></pre>

    <p>
      The integration works with any adapter. Swap <code>cloudflare()</code> for
      <code>node(&#123; mode: 'standalone' &#125;)</code> if you are deploying to a
      Node.js server.
    </p>

    <h2>Step 3 — Verify in dev mode</h2>

    <p>Start the dev server and test with <code>curl</code>:</p>

    <pre><code># Normal HTML response
curl http://localhost:4321/

# Markdown response — what AI agents receive
curl -H "Accept: text/markdown" http://localhost:4321/</code></pre>

    <p>
      The second request should return <code>Content-Type: text/markdown</code> and
      clean Markdown instead of HTML.
    </p>

    <h2>Optional — Custom agent detection</h2>

    <p>
      Pass <code>additionalAgents</code> to extend the built-in list with your own bot
      names:
    </p>

    <pre><code>markdownForAgents(&#123;
  additionalAgents: ['MyInternalCrawler', 'AnotherBot'],
&#125;)</code></pre>

    <p>
      Any <code>User-Agent</code> string that contains one of those substrings (case-
      insensitive) will trigger Markdown conversion.
    </p>

    <h2>How it works under the hood</h2>

    <p>
      The integration registers an Astro middleware via the
      <code>astro:config:setup</code> hook. A companion Vite plugin exposes your options
      through a virtual module (<code>virtual:astro-markdown-for-agents/config</code>)
      so the middleware can read them at both dev and build time without any file I/O.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      The HTML-to-Markdown converter uses pure regex, making it compatible with
      Cloudflare Workers and other edge runtimes that do not provide a DOM or Node.js
      APIs.
    </blockquote>

    <h2>Further reading</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/fundamentals/reference/markdown-for-agents/"
          >Cloudflare — Markdown for Agents</a
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/middleware/">Astro — Middleware</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://docs.astro.build/en/reference/integrations-reference/"
          >Astro — Integration API</a
        >
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Layout>
